# CLAUDE.md

Guidance for Claude Code when working in this repository. Generated by `acb init` — this file is
yours, and `acb pull` never touches it again.

## What this is

_One paragraph: what this project does, and the shape of it. A reader should be able to tell
whether a change belongs here from this paragraph alone._

## Development lifecycle

[`docs/sdlc.md`](docs/sdlc.md) is the full process — phases, gates, how it meets CI. It is
**carried** from the toolkit and byte-identical in every repository that uses it, so it is never
edited here.

**The process document is a contract:** if you change `.claude/skills/**`, `.claude/commands/**`,
any component's `verify.sh`, `.github/workflows/**`, or anything in `scripts/`, update it in the
same change. Which
document that is comes from `process.doc` in `.acb.json`, and the watched list from
`process.watched` beside it. Point `process.doc` at a local companion once this repository has
process specifics of its own: requiring an edit to the carried file would make this repository
permanently *ahead* of the toolkit, and `acb pull` would revert it. The `SDLC docs` CI job enforces
this; `[skip-sdlc-sync]` in the PR title is the escape hatch for a genuine no-op.

Which skill when — all vendored in `.claude/skills/`, loaded on demand:

| When | Skill |
| --- | --- |
| Requirements are vague or scope is unclear | `spec-driven-development` |
| Turning requirements into ordered tasks | `writing-plans` (plan review is mandatory) |
| Writing any logic, or fixing any bug | `test-driven-development` |
| Any change touching more than one file | `incremental-implementation` |
| Touching the sensitive paths below | `security-and-hardening` (threat-model first) |
| Tests fail, builds break, behaviour surprises | `debugging-and-error-recovery` |
| Committing, branching, versioning | `git-workflow-and-versioning` |
| A decision worth preserving; README/ADR upkeep | `documentation-and-adrs` (ADRs → `docs/adr/`) |
| Receiving review findings | `receiving-code-review` |

The standing bar every change clears is `.claude/skills/references/definition-of-done.md`.

One command ships alongside them: **`/loop-plan <plan path>`** works a plan to the criteria its
`## Criteria coverage` table claims, stops when they pass with evidence or when a
`Human dependencies` entry blocks, and never edits the plan. It is carried, like the skills.

**Tests: the oracle must not come from the implementation.** A test's oracle — whatever decides pass
from fail — has exactly three legal sources: written first, a document, or a second implementation.
Never ask for tests after the code; never edit an existing test to make it pass; mock only at
process boundaries, and _name this repository's boundaries below_. The full reasoning is in
[`docs/sdlc.md`](docs/sdlc.md) under *The oracle must not come from the implementation*; it is
summarised here because this file is loaded every session and that one is read on demand.

