## Components and commands

Each component has one `verify.sh` that CI invokes identically. Run it from that directory.

@@COMPONENT_TABLE@@

Targets come from `.acb.json`. The canonical vocabulary is `install`, `audit`, `lint`, `format`,
`typecheck`, `test`, `test:integration`, `build`, `package`, `migrate`, `publish`, `eval` and
`selftest`; a component declares the ones it has and may add its own. `SKIP_INSTALL=1` and
`SKIP_PACKAGE=1` speed up the inner loop — the pre-push run should be unskipped, because CI does
not skip.

## Review process

Every PR gets a code review and a security review against the pending diff, and every plan gets
the staff-engineer review the `writing-plans` skill dispatches. Neither is skipped because a
change looks small.

## CI job names are a contract

The ruleset requires status checks by job name. Renaming or removing a CI job breaks merges until
the ruleset matches. Change what runs *inside* a job freely; keep the name stable, or regenerate
`.github/ruleset.json` and re-run `scripts/apply-ruleset.sh` in the same change.

## Auto-merge setup

`.github/workflows/dependabot-auto-merge.yml` is present only if `.acb.json` declares at least one
ecosystem. It needs a GitHub App this repository does not have by default — create one with
`contents: write` and `pull-requests: write`, install it here, and set
`AUTOMERGE_APP_CLIENT_ID` / `AUTOMERGE_APP_PRIVATE_KEY` as secrets and `AUTOMERGE_APP_SLUG` as a
variable. The allow-list itself is the repository variable `ACB_DEPENDABOT_ECOSYSTEMS`, which
`acb init` sets and `acb status` reconciles against `.acb.json`. Until the App exists, the workflow
fails when it tries to mint a token — it merges nothing, which is the safe direction.

## Don't assume — surface it

If anything about a request is ambiguous, don't guess. State your assumptions and check first.
High-stakes ambiguity — architecture, security posture, scope, anything hard to reverse — is
always worth a question.
